@startuml
title TCG Pocket Battler — Architecture Overview

skinparam packageStyle rectangle
skinparam nodesep 25
skinparam ranksep 35

/'
  Read this diagram top to bottom: it is a stack of layers.

  The central idea is that a card's text is not code, it is DATA — an immutable
  tree of small composable nodes. Nothing in the "Card text" layer mutates
  anything; it is read and evaluated, and only IEffect is permitted to write to
  the runtime state. That split is what makes the model serializable, testable,
  and safe to seal.
'/

package "1. Drivers" #FFEBEE {
    class TurnEngine
    interface IPlayer
}

package "2. Runtime state — the only mutable things" #FFF3E0 {
    class Battle
    class Side
    class PokemonInPlay
}

package "3. Resolution — the ambient argument" #E8F5E9 {
    class ResolutionContext {
        - battle: Battle
        - controller: Side
        - source: PokemonInPlay
        - event: Optional<GameEvent>
        - scope: ResolutionScope
    }
}

package "4. Cards" #F3E5F5 {
    interface ICard <<sealed>>
    class CardInstance
}

package "5. Card text — immutable record trees" #E3F2FD {
    interface IAction <<sealed>> {
        + execute(ResolutionContext): AttemptResult
    }
    interface ITrigger <<sealed>> {
        + appliesTo(GameEvent): boolean
    }
    interface IAttempt <<sealed>> {
        + execute(ResolutionContext): AttemptResult
    }
    interface IEffect <<sealed>> {
        + apply(ResolutionContext): EffectOutcome
    }
    interface ITarget <<sealed>> {
        + resolve(ResolutionContext): PokemonInPlay
    }
    interface INumber <<sealed>> {
        + evaluate(ResolutionContext): int
    }
    interface "ICondition<T>" as ICondition <<sealed>> {
        + evaluate(T): boolean
    }
}

/' --- layer 1 -> 2 --- '/
TurnEngine *-- Battle       : drives phases
TurnEngine ..> IPlayer      : asks for decisions

/' --- layer 2 internals --- '/
Battle *-- "2" Side
Side   *-- PokemonInPlay    : active + bench
Side   *-- CardInstance     : hand / deck / discard

/' --- layer 3 --- '/
ResolutionContext o-- Battle
ResolutionContext o-- PokemonInPlay : "Self"

/' --- layer 4 --- '/
CardInstance <|-- PokemonInPlay
CardInstance o-- ICard      : definition
ICard *-- IAction

/' --- layer 5 internals: the composition chain --- '/
IAction  *-- IAttempt
ITrigger *-- IAttempt
IAttempt *-- IEffect        : ordered, short-circuits on FAILED
IEffect  *-- ITarget        : who
IEffect  *-- INumber        : how much
IAction  o-- ICondition     : preconditions
ITrigger o-- ICondition

note right of TurnEngine
  A turn is just `Decision<IAction>`:
  `Battle.legalActions(Side)` builds every legal
  move — attacks, retreat, evolve, play, attach,
  ability, end turn — and `IPlayer.choose` picks
  one. That single seam is what makes this a
  simulator rather than a card renderer, and it
  is the same seam used for every mid-resolution
  choice. See GameFlow.puml and IPlayer.puml.
end note

note right of ResolutionContext
  Replaces the bare `Battle` parameter everywhere.
  It answers three things a bare Battle cannot:
   * **who is "this"?** — a Tool's trigger means the
     Pokemon it is attached to, not whoever happens
     to be attacking (`Self`)
   * **what just happened?** — a trigger's attempt
     needs the event payload (`EventSource`, `EventDamage`)
   * **what did we just flip?** — coin results scoped to
     one resolution instead of a field on Battle
end note

note right of ICondition
  Every node in this layer is a **record** in a
  **sealed** hierarchy. What that buys:
   * exhaustive switch — a later card-text renderer
     or JSON writer needs no visitor and cannot
     silently miss a case
   * structural equality and toString for free,
     which test assertions lean on
   * trivially serializable, so the deferred JSON
     card DSL needs no reshaping of the model
end note

@enduml
